@attribute [Route(pageUrl)]
@layout DemosMainLayout

<PageMetaTags PageUrl="@pageUrl" Title="@metaTitle" Description="@metaDescription" ImageUrl="@imageUrl" />

<PageHero Title="@pageTitle">
    <SubTitleTemplate>
        @((MarkupString)pageDescription)
    </SubTitleTemplate>
</PageHero>

<DocsLink Href="@DemoRouteConstants.Docs_ScatterChart" />

<Prerequisites PageUrl="@pageUrl" />

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="How it works" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="how-it-works">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Scatter Chart</strong> component visualizes individual data points as (X, Y) coordinates, making it ideal for displaying relationships or distributions between two numeric variables.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add the <code>ScatterChart</code> component to your page and set its dimensions (e.g., <code>Width="560"</code>, <code>Height="480"</code>).</li>
                <li>Prepare your chart data by creating one or more <code>ScatterChartDataset</code> objects, each containing a list of <code>ScatterChartDataPoint</code> instances with X and Y values.</li>
                <li>Customize the appearance of each dataset using properties like <code>BackgroundColor</code>, <code>BorderColor</code>, and <code>BorderWidth</code>.</li>
                <li>Configure chart options using the <code>ScatterChartOptions</code> object as needed.</li>
                <li>Initialize the chart in <code>OnAfterRenderAsync</code> by calling <code>scatterChart.InitializeAsync(chartData, scatterChartOptions)</code>.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for practical examples of data preparation, dataset customization, and chart initialization.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        This demo shows how to set up a scatter chart, bind your data, and customize the chart's appearance to fit your application's needs.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(ScatterChart_Demo_01_Examples)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Dynamic data" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="dynamic-data">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Scatter Chart</strong> component supports dynamic data updates, allowing you to add, remove, or modify data points and datasets at runtime. This is useful for visualizing real-time data or enabling interactive data exploration.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add the <code>ScatterChart</code> component to your page and set its <code>Width</code> and <code>Height</code> as needed.</li>
                <li>Prepare your chart data using one or more <code>ScatterChartDataset</code> objects, each containing a list of <code>ScatterChartDataPoint</code> (with X and Y values).</li>
                <li>Implement methods to update your data dynamically, such as adding new points, randomizing values, or modifying existing datasets.</li>
                <li>After updating the data, call <code>scatterChart.UpdateAsync(chartData, scatterChartOptions)</code> to refresh the chart and reflect the changes.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for practical examples of dynamic data manipulation and chart updates in a Blazor application.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        This demo demonstrates how to work with dynamic data in a scatter chart, enabling interactive and real-time data visualizations in your Blazor projects.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(ScatterChart_Demo_02_Dynamic_Data)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Click event" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="click-event">
    <Block>
        Set the <code>OnClick</code> callback to react when a scatter point is selected. The callback receives <code>ChartClickEventArgs</code> with the dataset, point index, and raw coordinates; the demo displays the selected item below the chart.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(ScatterChart_Demo_03_Click_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Export image" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="export-image">
    <Block>
        Export the chart as an image with <code>ToBase64ImageAsync</code>, which returns a base64 data URL you can show, store or send, or save it straight to a file in the browser with <code>DownloadImageAsync</code>.
        If the chart is still animating, both wait for the animation to finish, and the image is streamed from the browser, so Blazor Server needs no larger message size.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Keep a reference to the chart with <code>@@ref</code> and initialize it.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ToBase64ImageAsync()</code> for a PNG data URL, or pass <code>ChartImageType.Jpeg</code> and a quality from 0 to 1.</li>
                <li>Call <code>DownloadImageAsync("scatter-chart.png")</code> to save the image as a file without sending it to .NET.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(ScatterChart_Demo_04_Export_Image)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Show and hide datasets" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="show-and-hide-datasets">
    <Block>
        Show and hide whole datasets or single points from C#, check whether a dataset is visible and count the visible datasets.
        Visibility changed at runtime, from C# or a legend click, stays in place when <code>UpdateAsync</code> refreshes the data, because datasets are matched by <code>Key</code>, label or order, and points by label.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>HideAsync(datasetIndex)</code> and <code>ShowAsync(datasetIndex)</code>, or <code>SetDatasetVisibilityAsync(datasetIndex, visible)</code>.</li>
                <li>Pass a data index too, for example <code>HideAsync(0, 2)</code>, to hide one point.</li>
                <li>Read the state with <code>IsDatasetVisibleAsync</code> and <code>GetVisibleDatasetCountAsync</code>.</li>
                <li>Refresh with <code>UpdateAsync(data, options, ChartUpdateMode.None)</code> to apply new data without animation; hidden datasets and points stay hidden.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(ScatterChart_Demo_05_Show_Hide_Datasets)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Legend click event" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="legend-click-event">
    <Block>
        Set <code>OnLegendClick</code> to react when a legend item is clicked. The callback receives <code>ChartLegendClickEventArgs</code> with the dataset index, the legend text and whether the dataset is hidden after the click.
        Set <code>LegendClickToggles="false"</code> to stop the legend from hiding datasets, for example to open details instead.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add an <code>OnLegendClick</code> callback to the chart.</li>
                <li>Keep <code>LegendClickToggles</code> at <code>true</code> for the default hide and show, or set it to <code>false</code> to handle the click yourself.</li>
                <li>Use <code>DatasetIndex</code>, <code>Text</code> and <code>Hidden</code> from the event arguments.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(ScatterChart_Demo_06_Legend_Click_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Hover event" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="hover-event">
    <Block>
        Set <code>OnHover</code> to react when the pointer moves onto a different point. The callback receives <code>ChartHoverEventArgs</code> with the same item details as <code>OnClick</code>.
        It is called only when the hovered point changes, and once with a <code>null</code> <code>DatasetIndex</code> when the pointer leaves all points, so it does not flood Blazor Server with calls.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add an <code>OnHover</code> callback to the chart.</li>
                <li>Check <code>DatasetIndex</code>: a value means a point is hovered; <code>null</code> means the pointer left all points.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(ScatterChart_Demo_07_Hover_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Programmatic highlight" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="programmatic-highlight">
    <Block>
        Highlight points and show their tooltip from C# with <code>SetActiveElementsAsync</code>, for example to point at a value from a table next to the chart.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Pass one or more <code>ChartActiveElement(datasetIndex, index)</code> items to <code>SetActiveElementsAsync</code>.</li>
                <li>Pass an empty list to clear the highlight and the tooltip.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(ScatterChart_Demo_08_Programmatic_Highlight)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Update modes and animation control" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="update-modes-and-animation-control">
    <Block>
        Choose how <code>UpdateAsync</code> animates with <code>ChartUpdateMode</code>, and control the running animation with <code>StopAsync</code>, <code>RenderAsync</code>, <code>ResetAsync</code> and <code>ClearAsync</code>.
        These map one-to-one to the Chart.js <code>update(mode)</code>, <code>stop()</code>, <code>render()</code>, <code>reset()</code> and <code>clear()</code> methods.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>UpdateAsync(data, options)</code> for the default animated update, or pass <code>ChartUpdateMode.None</code> to apply the change at once. An animated update moves each matched dataset from its current values to the new ones, and a new dataset grows in. Datasets are matched by <code>Key</code> when set, otherwise by a unique <code>Label</code>, otherwise by order.</li>
                <li>Call <code>StopAsync()</code> to end any running animation; the chart stays as it was drawn at that moment. Call <code>UpdateAsync</code> again to animate to new values.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ResetAsync()</code> to put the chart back in its starting state, before its first animation, and <code>RenderAsync()</code> to draw that state. Stop any running animation first, or its next frame draws over the reset. Call <code>ResetAsync()</code> and then <code>UpdateAsync(data, options)</code> to regrow the chart from its starting state, as on first render.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ClearAsync()</code> to clear the canvas; <code>RenderAsync()</code> draws the chart again. A running animation draws over the cleared canvas on its next frame, so stop it first or wait for it to end.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(ScatterChart_Demo_09_Update_Modes)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

@code {
    private const string pageUrl = DemoRouteConstants.Demos_ScatterChart;
    private const string pageTitle = "Scatter Chart";
    private const string pageDescription = "The <code>Blazor Scatter Chart</code> component displays data values as vertical bars, making it easy to compare multiple data sets or visualize trends over time.";
    private const string metaTitle = "Blazor Scatter Chart";
    private const string metaDescription = "The Blazor Scatter Chart component displays data values as vertical bars, making it easy to compare multiple data sets or visualize trends over time.";
    private const string imageUrl = DemoImageSrcConstants.ScatterChart;
}
